Subject: faster JSON.stringify for keys, strings and numbers

What this does
  Speeds up JSON.stringify on the paths apps exercise most, without
  changing what is emitted.

What it changes
  - Non-exotic objects enumerate own enumerable string keys as atoms
    directly instead of building a JS array of key strings and reading them
    back out (JS_GetOwnPropertyNamesInternal).
  - Quoting is split into json_quote_str, which writes into the caller's
    buffer. Keys and string values quote straight from the JSString instead
    of building an intermediate quoted string to copy and free.
  - The array and object branches build the key string only when a replacer
    function or a toJSON lookup could observe it.
  - Float, boolean and null values write directly into the buffer. Integral
    doubles format as their digits, exact within +/-2^53, avoiding
    multi-precision arithmetic. -0 prints "0", as Number(-0).toString()
    gives.

Why we need it
  JSON.stringify is on the hot path of every app that talks to a network:
  fetch bodies, cached payloads, bridge round trips. The old code built a
  key array, an intermediate quoted string per key and per string value,
  and a JSString per number.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No. This changes only runtime behaviour inside quickjs.c; the bytecode
  format is untouched.

Notes
  Ported from the research tree (0010-json-stringify-fast-paths) and layered
  on 0008, which supplies the JS_ToQuotedString narrow fast path this
  refactor splits out. The source's toJSON skip is not carried over -- 0008
  deliberately removed that optimization for a correctness bug, and this
  patch must look up toJSON on every object.

  An earlier draft of the research patch had progress comments throughout;
  only the integral-double exactness note is kept here.
